/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - f1 = fc + Rb/4, f2 = fc − Rb/4
   - fc is the centre (carrier) frequency and Rb = 1/Tb the bit rate.
   - Rb/2 is the smallest spacing at which two tones are coherently orthogonal over a bit (see BFSK); hence "minimum" shift.
   - Write the signal as s(t) = A cos(2πfc t + θ(t)).
   - s(t) = A[aI(t)cos(πt/2Tb)cos(2πfc t) − aQ(t)sin(πt/2Tb)sin(2πfc t)]
   - aI and aQ are ±1 streams built from alternate bits; each value lasts 2Tb, and the Q stream is offset from the I stream by Tb.
   - So MSK is offset QPSK with half-sine pulses instead of rectangles: because the two rails change at different instants and each pulse eases in and out, the envelope stays exactly constant.
   - The MSK spectrum has a main lobe 1.5Rb wide between nulls, against Rb for QPSK and 2Rb for BPSK, but its first side lobe is 23 dB down (13 dB for QPSK) and the lobes fall as 1/f⁴ instead of 1/f². 99% of the MSK power lies within about 1.2Rb
   - B is the 3 dB bandwidth of the Gaussian lowpass filter in Hz; BT is a pure number.
   - The filter sits before the frequency modulator, so the output is still constant-envelope MSK-like FM.
*/
